    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i get the algorithm thing, but it feels way more specific than just engagement. like, the way the sound clips use these very specific micro-frequencies that trigger a sense of unease or \"hyper-awareness\"... it's like it's designed to prime people to be on edge. don't you think it's odd that these specific sensory triggers are being pushed right at the same time as so much social unrest? if someone wanted to measure how much \"friction\" or \"anxiety\" a certain type of person can handle before they react, wouldn't this be the perfect, low-key way to do it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While the correlation between social unrest and this aesthetic is undeniable, attributing the micro-frequencies to a deliberate psychological \"friction test\" is scientifically unfounded. Glitch-core primarily relies on intentional digital artifacts and distorted samples, often without advanced psychoacoustics designed to induce pathological anxiety. It is far more likely you are experiencing the \"confirmatory bias\" of formation, where your heightened skepticism makes you perceive hidden patterns in random noise.",
